    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for that. so if the ldl is building up in the arteries, does it like, actively damage the walls or just sit there? i'm trying to visualize how it actually causes problems over time.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "LDL cholesterol can't actually \"actively damage\" arteries walls, but it can contribute to inflammation and endothelial dysfunction, which can lead to damage over time. When LDL builds up in arteries, it forms plaques, which can lead to the narrowing and hardening of blood vessels, ultimately increasing blood pressure and cardiac workload. This can cause cracks in the arterial wall, allowing cholesterol and other substances to leak into the bloodstream, further exacerbating the problem.",
      "source": "target",
